/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  pump04_manufacturer_curve
  =========================

  A REAL PUMP: the maker's characteristic curve is a measured RECORD of the
  case (DEV.md 4c C441, level 2).  The pump does not guess an efficiency and
  is not given a pressure: it sits on its curve.  At the flow the feed
  carries (about 45 m3/h) the engine reads, off constant/experiments/
  illustrativeCurve.dat,
      H(Q)      the head the pump delivers  -> P_out = P_in + rho g H
      eta(Q)    the efficiency              -> W_shaft = Q dP / eta
      NPSHr(Q)  the NPSH required (NPSH3)   -> the margin against the NPSHA
  and prints them.  A flow outside the curve's range is REFUSED by name (the
  engine never extrapolates a maker's curve).

  THE CURVE IN THIS CASE IS ILLUSTRATIVE.  It was constructed for this
  tutorial to have the shape of a small single-stage pump's curve; it is
  NOT a manufacturer's curve and must not be used to size a real pump.  The
  record says so (`kind illustrative`) and the run prints it.  A real record
  has `kind measured` and cites the data sheet (and the test standard the
  curve was taken to, ISO 9906).  Replace the file's rows with a data
  sheet's and the case becomes that pump's.

  The margin rule is Gulich, Centrifugal Pumps, 4th ed., Table 6.2, p. 379:
  NPSHA >= max(1.25 NPSH3, NPSH3 + 0.6 m); the NPSHA is Eq. T2.2.8, p. 52,
  the same as in the generic level (pump03_generic_selection).

  Not applied: speed and impeller-trim corrections, and the correction of the
  curve for a viscous liquid (Gulich Ch. 13); the head in metres is used as
  read, for a liquid of water's viscosity.  Hand check at 45 m3/h: the rows
  at 40 and 50 m3/h give H = (67+63)/2 = 65 m, eta = (0.72+0.74)/2 = 0.73,
  NPSHr = (2.9+3.7)/2 = 3.3 m; dP = 997*9.80665*65 = 6.36 bar.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

// stream state lives on disk in 0/ (per-stream files); flowsheetDict is TOPOLOGY only

units
(
    {
        name        pump04;
        type        pump;
        in          feed;
        outputs     ( out );

        operation
        {
            curve       illustrativeCurve;   // constant/experiments/illustrativeCurve.dat

            suction
            {
                staticHead  -2 m;            // z_e: the pump is 2 m ABOVE the liquid level (a lift)
                lineLoss    0.5 m;           // H_v,s: loss in the suction line
            }
        }
    }
);
